<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohéli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mwali, Moheli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mohéli, or Mwali, is the smallest of the four main islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-comore">Grande Comore</a> and Anjouan. Long claimed by the sultans of Anjouan, it was seized around 1830 by the Malagasy prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanetaka">Ramanetaka</a>, whose family ruled it as sultans until it became a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1886. It is now an autonomous island of the Union of the Comoros, known for its marine park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moheli.jpg" alt="Mohéli" width="262" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moh%C3%A9li_in_Comoros.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mohéli, also called Mwali, lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and is the smallest of the four major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoro Islands</a>. Its capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fomboni">Fomboni</a>, where about 19,000 of the island's roughly 38,000 people lived at a count of 2006. Its main ethnic group, as on the other Comoro islands, is the Comorian people, a synthesis of Bantu, Arab, Malay and Malagasy culture, and its main religion is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until 1830 Mohéli was part of the Ndzuwani Sultanate, which also controlled the neighbouring island of Anjouan. In 1830 migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanetaka">Ramanetaka</a>, who later changed his name to Abderemane, took over the island and established the sultanate of Mwali. Its ruler in the mid-nineteenth century was Queen Jumbe-Souli. In 1886 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> made the island a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. Mwali kept its own French resident until 1889, when it was placed under the residency of Anjouan, and the sultanate was dismantled in 1909 after the French annexation of the island.</p><p>In 1975 Mohéli agreed to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-comore">Grande Comore</a> and Anjouan. Political, economic and social turmoil followed. On 11 August 1997 Mohéli seceded, a week after Anjouan, under the leaders Said Mohamed Soefu, who became president, and Soidri Ahmed, who became prime minister. It rejoined the Comoros in 1998. In 2002 it ratified a new Comorian constitution that provided for a less centralised federal government and more power to the island governments, which helped to settle the turmoil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mohéli Marine Park, gazetted on 19 April 2001, was the first protected area in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>. It came out of an arrangement negotiated between the government and the communities of the ten villages around its boundaries. It was redesignated Mohéli National Park in 2010 and in 2015 was enlarged to cover about three-quarters of the island's land area. A tract of 6,268 hectares in the western highlands, including Mount Mlédjélé, is an Important Bird Area for species such as the Comoro olive pigeon and the Moheli scops owl. The island's only airport lies between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fomboni">Fomboni</a> and Djoièzi on the north coast.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mohéli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ndzwani and <b>Mwali</b> declared independence in 1997, and Azali Assoumani seized power in 1999.</blockquote><a href="/state/comoros">Comoros</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultans claimed overlordship of Mayotte and <b>Moheli</b> and fought repeatedly to enforce it.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-anjouan">Sultanate of Anjouan</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1830 Ramanetaka, a Merina prince and governor who had fled Madagascar, seized control of <b>Moheli</b> (Mwali) and set himself up as sultan with the Muslim name Abderremane, displacing the island's earlier rulers.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-moheli">Sultanate of Moheli</a></li></ul></section>
